 "rulebook": "Official Baseball Rules (MLB, 2024 edition): 2.01 and Appendices 1-2 (the pitcher's plate 60 ft 6 in from the back point of home plate, 10 in above it; the mound), 2.02 (home plate), 3.01 (the ball), 5.07(a) (the pivot foot in contact with the plate), 5.07(c) (the 15 s pitch timer), Definitions of Terms: \"strike zone\", \"in flight\"",
 "deviations": [
  "3.01: the hand holds the ball by a grip switched on and off, not by fingers (no seams: no grip-made spin beyond what the hand's motion gives); the ball is released when the grip is switched off.",
  "Definitions (strike zone): there is no batter, so the zone's height is fixed, 0.46 m to 1.07 m above home plate (the hollow of the knee to the midpoint of the torso of a 1.8 m batter). A strike is called when the ball's centre crosses the zone's front plane (home plate's front edge) inside the zone, in flight; the rule's \"any part of the ball through any part of the zone\" (the plate's whole depth, the ball's edge) is not used: stricter.",
  "5.07(a): \"the pivot foot in contact with the plate\" is judged at the release: a foot that touches the mound and whose sole is over the plate; either foot may be the pivot foot. Wind-up and set positions and balks are not judged (no runners).",
  "5.07(c): the pitch timer runs from the start of the episode: the ball must be let go within 15.0 s.",
  "No batter, catcher or umpire: a backstop behind home plate; one pitch, and a fall before the call ends the attempt as a failure.",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Pitch a strike: from the pitcher's plate on the mound, with a foot on the plate, throw the baseball in your right hand through the strike zone over home plate, on the fly.",
  "rules": "The baseball (0.073 m across, 0.145 kg, it flies in air with drag and spin lift) starts gripped in your right hand. You stand on the pitcher's plate (a slab 0.61 m wide and 0.152 m deep, level with the mound's top) on a mound 5.486 m across whose top is 0.254 m above home plate; in front of the plate the mound falls 1 in 12. The front edge of the pitcher's plate is 18.44 m from the back point of home plate; home plate is 0.432 m wide and as deep, so its front edge is that much nearer. The strike zone is the rectangle above home plate's front edge, 0.432 m wide and from 0.46 m to 1.07 m above home plate. The pitch: you let go of the ball (once: it cannot be gripped again) within 15.0 s of the start, and at that moment at least one of your feet is in contact with the pitcher's plate (it touches the mound and part of its sole is over the plate). You succeed when the ball's centre then crosses the plane of the zone inside the zone, before the ball has touched the ground. You fail if no foot is on the plate at the release; if the ball is not let go in time; if it touches the ground (the mound included) before home plate; if it crosses the plane outside the zone (a ball); if any part of you touches it after the release (your throwing arm may still brush it during the first 0.2 s); if you fall before the call (your pelvis lower than 0.75 m above home plate's level, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground or the mound); or after 20 s.",
  "venue": "You stand on the mound facing home plate (+x), both feet across the pitcher's plate, your pelvis above its middle; home plate's front edge is 18.1 m ahead and 0.254 m below the plate you stand on. The mound's level top reaches 0.15 m in front of the pitcher's plate, 0.56 m behind it and 0.76 m to each side. A backstop stands 3 m behind home plate. The ball is against your right palm, the forearm level and pointing forwards. Still air. The ball is released when the hand's grip is switched off.",
  "difficulty": "From 18.44 m the ball needs more than 13 m/s even on a high arc, and the zone is 0.43 m wide: a release direction right to about one degree. The H1-2 has no throwing motion; the speed has to come from the trunk and the whole arm while a foot stays on the plate and the body on the mound."
 },
